**Severity:** high (the #333/#483/#531 guard that stops `warehouse_row` from being an existence oracle for personal data is absent for ClickHouse; the topology trace can read another tenant's row)

**Where:** `internal/clients/clickhouse/warehouse.go:53-66` (`Row` quotes whatever `pkColumn` it gets: ``SELECT * FROM %s%s WHERE %s = ? LIMIT 1``); no `PrimaryKeyColumn` method exists in the package. Callers treat it as optional: `internal/tools/dbtools/wh.go:147-151` ("Checked when the client reports the key") and `internal/topology/trace.go:355-358` (`checkSinkKey`: `d, ok := b.wh.(pkColumner); if !ok { return nil }`). `internal/wiring/warehouse.go:49-53` lists `PrimaryKeyColumn` among the readers kept visible through the opener. Introduced by PR #697 / #706 (`c3/clickhouse-client`, `ch2-warehouse-wiring`).

**Scenario:** With `WAREHOUSE_KIND=clickhouse`, the agent (or a steered driver) calls `warehouse_row` with `pk_column=email`, `key=jane@example.com`. Postgres, Snowflake, BigQuery and Databricks refuse this with `ErrBadInput`. On ClickHouse the lookup runs and answers `found: yes, updated_at=…`, and the probed value lands in the room log. The trace has the same hole: for a ClickHouse sink with `ORDER BY (tenant_id, id)` the sink row is looked up by the source key alone (the #537 scenario), so another tenant's row feeds `diff_columns` and the timestamps.

**Reproduction:** throwaway tests (deleted after the run).
```go
// internal/clients/clickhouse/qa_wh_test.go (package clickhouse)
func TestQA_RowByAnyColumnWithoutPrimaryKeyColumn(t *testing.T) {
	type pkColumner interface{ PrimaryKeyColumn(ctx context.Context, table string) (string, error) }
	if _, ok := any((*Client)(nil)).(pkColumner); !ok {
		t.Errorf("*clickhouse.Client does not implement PrimaryKeyColumn: warehouse_row cannot check pk_column against the table key")
	}
	q := &fakeQuerier{responses: []queryResponse{{rows: &fakeRows{
		columns: []string{"id", "email", "updated_at"},
		data:    [][]any{{int64(7), "jane@example.com", "2026-10-22 10:00:00"}}}}}}
	c := newTestClient(q)
	row, found, err := c.Row(context.Background(), "analytics.customers", "email", "jane@example.com")
	if err != nil { t.Fatal(err) }
	t.Logf("found=%v row=%v", found, row)
	t.Logf("SQL sent: %s args=%v", q.calls[0].query, q.calls[0].args)
}
```
```go
// internal/tools/dbtools/qa_wh_test.go (package dbtools): a clients.Warehouse without PrimaryKeyColumn, as the ClickHouse client is
type noPKWarehouse struct{ inner *fakeWarehouse }
func (w noPKWarehouse) Row(ctx context.Context, table, pk, key string) (map[string]any, bool, error) { return w.inner.Row(ctx, table, pk, key) }
func (w noPKWarehouse) Freshness(ctx context.Context, table, col string) (time.Time, error) { return w.inner.fresh, nil }
func TestQA_WarehouseRowByEmailOnClickHouse(t *testing.T) {
	d := testDeps()
	fw := d.Wh.(*fakeWarehouse)
	fw.rows["analytics.orders|jane@example.com"] = map[string]any{"id": "7", "email": "jane@example.com", "updated_at": "2026-10-11T10:00:00Z"}
	d.Wh, d.WarehouseKind = noPKWarehouse{inner: fw}, "clickhouse"
	res, err := runTool(t, whRowTool(t, d), `{"pipeline_id":"orders","table":"analytics.orders","pk_column":"email","key":"jane@example.com"}`)
	t.Logf("err=%v summary=%q rowCalls=%d", err, res.Summary, fw.rowCalls)
	if err == nil { t.Errorf("warehouse_row by a non-key column was answered on a clickhouse warehouse") }
}
```
